#: lib/potok_ide_web/stack_copy.ex:74
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "When something goes wrong, only that process falls. Its page reconnects on its own and nobody else notices. A supervisor starts the permanent parts of the app and brings them back if they stop."
msgstr ""

#: lib/potok_ide_web/stack_copy.ex:300
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "Where Tailwind stops"
msgstr ""

#: lib/potok_ide_web/stack_copy.ex:330
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "`chat-bubble` is a message bubble, `drawer` the side menu, `dropdown` a drop-down menu, `toast` a note at the bottom of the screen. It is all plain CSS, so it does not get in the way of LiveView, which updates the page by itself."
msgstr ""

#: lib/potok_ide_web/stack_copy.ex:322
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "daisyUI is a plugin for Tailwind. It gives the common parts of an interface as named classes and adds no JavaScript."
msgstr ""

#: lib/potok_ide_web/stack_copy.ex:218
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "When someone saves a value, Phoenix PubSub publishes it on the group's topic, and every page process listening there gets it and shows it. Unread counts have a topic of their own, so one value is one publication, however many members the group has. So do the values apps keep under rules, and each page decides by those rules whether its reader may see one."
msgstr ""

#: lib/potok_ide_web/stack_copy.ex:106
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "ETS is an in-memory table that every process can read. Potok keeps in it what is expensive to compute and rarely changes: rendered Markdown and the access token for notifications. The pictures it draws, link cards and avatars, go to files on disk instead, so they take no memory. ETS also counts log-in attempts and the emails someone asks Potok to send, so nobody can repeat them without end."
msgstr ""

#: lib/potok_ide_web/stack_copy.ex:82
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "Each process cleans its own memory, so cleaning never stops the whole server. Potok makes use of that: a page's connection does a full sweep every time, both are swept once more two seconds after loading, and after that the page looks at its connection every 20 seconds and has it swept if it has grown. A page that receives nothing for two seconds shrinks to what it really holds. That is how a small server holds many open pages."
msgstr ""

#: lib/potok_ide_web/stack_copy.ex:148
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "PostGIS knows places on the Earth"
msgstr ""

#: lib/potok_ide_web/stack_copy.ex:150
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "PostgreSQL runs PostGIS, its extension for places on the Earth. It checks that the border of an area does not cross itself, finds the areas that hold a group's pin or that a route passes through by an index instead of reading every border, measures a route's length along the Earth's surface, and simplifies a border or a route for each zoom of a map, so a phone gets only the points it can show."
msgstr ""

#: lib/potok_ide_web/stack_copy.ex:36
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "Everything here runs at Fly.io: the app, the database and the files. See this month so far and every month before, estimated and billed."
msgstr ""

#: lib/potok_ide_web/stack_copy.ex:90
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "Notifications go out from processes of their own, so a page does not wait for the delivery services. They wait in one line and go two at a time, so a run of posts does not start hundreds of deliveries at once. Event reminders are a process that wakes up every half minute, and so are the deadlines of polls. Once an hour another one deletes the files apps took in that no answer claimed. The pictures for link cards and for the avatars in notifications are drawn one at a time, each in a process of its own, and at most three link previews are fetched at once. Every quarter of an hour one more asks Fly.io what runs and writes down what that hour cost, for the costs page. None of this needs a separate job queue. One more process keeps the borders of areas and the lines of routes a map asked for, simplified, and lets go of those used longest ago."
msgstr ""

